    "body": "`CHMAC_SHA256` and `CHMAC_SHA512` do not wipe their two hasher objects when they are destroyed. After `Finalize()` these still hold the HMAC output and the last bytes of the message, so `CExtKey::SetSeed()` leaves the seed and the master key behind, and `BIP32Hash()` for a hardened child leaves the parent private key. `CreateMuSig2PartialSig()` also leaves a `secp256k1_keypair`, which contains the secret key, on the stack.\n\nI add destructors to both HMAC classes that `memory_cleanse()` the two hashers, following #35254, which cleansed `rkey` and `temp` in the same files. The MuSig2 keypair now comes from `CKey::ComputeKeyPair()`, which keeps it in secure memory. I checked with a small program, built from these files, that the seed and key bytes are in the HMAC object after `Finalize()` and gone after its destructor.\n",
